# Baggage Policy

## Overview

SkyDesk displays baggage allowances supplied by the airline operating
each flight. Baggage rules can vary by airline, route, cabin, fare
family, and passenger type. The allowance shown in the booking
confirmation is the allowance attached to that specific booking and
takes priority over general examples in this guide.

Customers should check their booking before travelling because
purchasing a higher cabin class does not always mean that checked
baggage is included. Some economy and promotional fares include only a
personal item or cabin bag.

## Personal Items

A personal item is a small bag designed to fit under the seat in front
of the passenger. Typical examples include a handbag, small backpack,
laptop bag, or camera bag.

Unless a booking states otherwise, one personal item per passenger is
permitted. The recommended maximum size is 40 × 30 × 15 cm.

Personal items must not block the aisle or emergency exits. Airport
staff may require an oversized personal item to be checked into the hold
and additional charges may apply.

## Cabin Baggage

Where cabin baggage is included, the standard SkyDesk reference
allowance is one cabin bag up to 55 × 40 × 23 cm and 8 kg. The airline's
rules shown on the booking remain authoritative.

Cabin baggage must fit safely in an overhead compartment. Space in the
cabin is not guaranteed. On full flights, an airline may place otherwise
compliant cabin baggage in the hold at the gate.

Customers should keep travel documents, medication, keys, money,
valuables, batteries, and essential electronics in their personal item
when possible.

## Checked Baggage

Checked baggage is not included in every fare. Customers can see the
included allowance in the booking details under **Baggage**.

Common allowances are:

-   Basic fares: no checked baggage.
-   Standard fares: one checked bag up to 23 kg on participating
    airlines.
-   Flexible and premium fares: one or more checked bags depending on
    the airline and route.

A single checked bag must normally not exceed 32 kg, even when a
passenger has a larger total weight allowance. Bags above the airline's
individual bag limit may need to be repacked.

## Adding Baggage

When supported by the airline, customers can add baggage through **My
Trips → Manage booking → Baggage**.

Additional baggage is subject to availability and airline pricing.
Prices may change between booking and purchase. Airport baggage fees are
often higher than advance online prices.

If SkyDesk cannot sell additional baggage for a booking, the customer
should purchase it directly from the operating airline.

## Connecting Flights

For itineraries containing multiple airlines, baggage rules can differ
between flight segments. SkyDesk shows the baggage allowance available
from the booking data, but customers should review the allowance for
each segment.

When flights are issued on separate tickets, baggage normally must be
collected and checked in again during the connection. The passenger is
responsible for allowing enough connection time.

## Infants and Children

Child baggage allowance generally follows the fare purchased. Infant
allowances vary considerably by airline.

A collapsible stroller or child car seat may be accepted without charge
by many airlines, but this is not guaranteed. Customers travelling with
infant equipment should check the airline-specific conditions before
departure.

## Sports Equipment and Special Baggage

Bicycles, skis, surfboards, golf bags, musical instruments, mobility
equipment, and other oversized items are not treated as standard
baggage.

Customers should request special baggage as early as possible.
Acceptance may depend on aircraft capacity and airline approval. Payment
alone does not guarantee acceptance unless confirmation has been issued.

## Restricted Items

Dangerous goods, explosives, flammable materials, and other prohibited
items must not be packed in baggage. Spare lithium batteries and power
banks should generally be carried in cabin baggage rather than checked
baggage.

Security and dangerous-goods rules are determined by airlines, airports,
and authorities. Customers must follow the rules applicable to their
itinerary.

## Delayed, Damaged, or Missing Baggage

Baggage is handled by the airline and airport baggage provider, not by
SkyDesk.

A passenger whose checked baggage is missing or damaged should report
the issue at the airline's baggage desk before leaving the airport and
obtain a Property Irregularity Report (PIR) or equivalent reference.

SkyDesk support can help identify the operating airline and provide
booking documents, but cannot locate baggage directly or approve baggage
compensation.
